<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Brycheiniog</h1><p class="st-meta">450 CE – 1045 CE (approx.) · capital Llangorse</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Welsh kingdom of the Usk valley, founded according to legend by the Irish-descended Brychan, whose royal crannog at Llangorse was stormed by the Mercians in 916.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Brycheiniog took its name, by tradition, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brychan">Brychan</a>, a legendary 5th-century ruler of Irish and British descent whose many saintly children are celebrated in Welsh and Cornish tradition. Ogham-inscribed stones in the region support Irish settlement in the post-Roman period. The kingdom occupied the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-usk">Usk</a> valley and the uplands around modern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-brecknock">Brecon</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its kings were often under pressure from larger neighbours. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asser">Asser</a> records that Elisedd ap Tewdwr submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred of Wessex</a> in the 880s. The royal crannog on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llangorse-lake">Llangorse Lake</a>, an artificial island built in the 890s, is the only one known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>; in 916 the Mercians under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelflaed">Æthelflæd</a> attacked it and carried off the king's wife. Tewdwr ab Elisedd later witnessed English royal charters.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Brycheiniog lost its independence in the 11th century, coming under the rulers of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-deheubarth">Deheubarth</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-morgannwg">Morgannwg</a>. In 1093 the Norman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernard-de-neufmarche">Bernard de Neufmarché</a> defeated and killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhys-ap-tewdwr">Rhys ap Tewdwr</a> near <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-brecknock">Brecon</a> and created the Norman lordship of Brecknock, which later became the county of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brecknockshire">Brecknockshire</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-brycheiniog.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Brycheiniog" width="320" height="421"><figcaption>Image: Owj20 at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Medieval_Wales.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>450 CE – 1045 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Llangorse</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Elisedd ap Tewdwr, Tewdwr ab Elisedd</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Welsh</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Brycheiniog, Brecknock</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=748&amp;focus=kingdom-of-brycheiniog" aria-label="Kingdom of Brycheiniog on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4604 382 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Brycheiniog lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4924 638L4909 640M4924 638L4924 638"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4909 640h0M4924 638h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4924 638h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4924 638h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=748&amp;focus=kingdom-of-brycheiniog">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
